      minWords: 230, use: ["subtext", "juxtaposition", "not only", "read between the lines"],
      model: "In the final chapter of Chekhov's 'The Lady with the Dog', very little seems to happen. Gurov and Anna meet secretly in a hotel room in Moscow; she cries, he orders tea, and they talk about how to escape their double lives. On the surface, the scene is almost static. Yet it is precisely this stillness that makes it one of the most powerful endings in Russian literature.\n\nNot only does Chekhov refuse to tell us what the lovers will do, but he also denies us any dramatic gesture. There is no confession, no quarrel, no decision. Instead, the meaning lies in the subtext. When Gurov catches sight of himself in the mirror and notices that his hair is turning grey, the reader understands, without being told, that time is running out and that this love has arrived too late to be lived freely.\n\nThe most striking device is the juxtaposition of the trivial and the profound. Gurov calmly drinks his tea while Anna is in tears, and this contrast reveals both his tenderness and his helplessness. Chekhov trusts us to read between the lines: the tea is not indifference but an attempt to hold on to ordinary life in the middle of an impossible situation.\n\nFinally, the open ending forces the reader to take part in the story. The last sentence tells us that the most complicated and difficult part is only just beginning. Rarely has a writer said so much by apparently saying so little, and rarely has an ending felt so much like a beginning."
