      model: "Kazuo Ishiguro's 'The Remains of the Day' is narrated by Stevens, an ageing English butler who sets off on a motoring trip in the summer of 1956. Ostensibly, the novel is a travelogue punctuated by memories of his years in service at Darlington Hall. In reality, it is one of the finest portraits of an unreliable narrator in modern fiction.\n\nStevens does not lie, exactly. What he does is far more subtle: he arranges the past so that he never has to face it. His tone is formal, measured and almost comically restrained, and it is precisely this restraint that gives him away. Much as he insists on his professional pride, the reader gradually realises that he has sacrificed his chance of love, and much of his moral judgement, to an employer who did not deserve his loyalty.\n\nIshiguro's great achievement is to let the reader see what Stevens cannot. There are moments of wry humour, such as his earnest attempts to master the art of 'bantering', but they are always tinged with sadness. In hindsight, Stevens half-admits that he may have made mistakes, yet even this admission is wrapped in so many qualifications that it almost disappears.\n\nStrange though it may seem, the novel's most powerful scenes are those in which nothing is said. When Miss Kenton tells him that she is leaving to get married, Stevens simply returns to his duties. Rarely has silence been so eloquent. This is a book that rewards slow, attentive reading, and it stays with you long after the last page."
