      text: "Every few decades, someone announces the death of the sonnet. It is too rigid, the argument goes, too bound up with courtly love and Elizabethan ruffs to say anything about the way we live now. And every few decades, the sonnet calmly outlives its obituary.\n\nThe form is deceptively simple. In its English version, a sonnet consists of fourteen lines of iambic pentameter: ten syllables per line, alternating unstressed and stressed beats, like a heart that is slightly out of breath. Three quatrains are followed by a rhyming couplet, and the rhyme scheme, ABAB CDCD EFEF GG, gives the poem a sense of mounting pressure that the final two lines release. The Italian version, which Petrarch made famous in the fourteenth century, divides the poem differently, into eight lines and six, and places the crucial turn, or volta, between them.\n\nIt is the volta that explains the form's astonishing staying power. A sonnet is not a statement but an argument with itself. In Shakespeare's eighteenth sonnet, for instance, the speaker begins by asking whether he should compare his beloved to a summer's day, spends twelve lines dismantling the comparison, and then, in the couplet, pivots to a far bolder claim: that the poem itself will keep the beloved alive 'so long as men can breathe or eyes can see'. The conceit is outrageous, and it has turned out to be true.\n\nModern poets have treated the form with a mixture of reverence and mischief. Some keep the metre but abandon rhyme; others keep the fourteen lines and throw out everything else. Enjambment, in which a sentence spills over the end of one line into the next, allows a poet to strain against the form's boundaries, so that the reader feels both the cage and the attempt to escape it. A well-placed caesura, a pause in the middle of a line, can do the same work as a sharp intake of breath.\n\nI first understood this at the age of seventeen, when a teacher made our class read a sonnet aloud, one line each. I had always found poetry faintly embarrassing; I had assumed that rhyme belonged to greeting cards and doggerel. But as the lines moved round the room, something happened. The alliteration in one line made a boy stumble; the sibilance of another came out as a whisper; and when the volta arrived, the girl who read it paused without being told to, as though the poem had tapped her on the shoulder. I was never to look at fourteen lines in quite the same way again.\n\nThat, perhaps, is the real secret. A sonnet is small enough to memorise and strict enough to resist us, and in the tension between those two qualities lies its music. The form survives not because poets are nostalgic but because, like any good constraint, it forces language to work harder. Free verse can do many things. What it rarely does is make a single line feel as though it has been earned.",
