      text: "What is a translator? A traitor, says the old Italian pun: traduttore, traditore. A servant, say publishers, who would prefer us to remain invisible. A thief, say some authors, who resent seeing their sentences dismantled and reassembled in a language they cannot read. After twenty years of translating Russian fiction into English, I have come to believe that a translator is all of these things, and something else besides: a writer who has agreed not to have any ideas of her own.\n\nThe first lesson every translator learns is that fidelity is not the same as accuracy. A verbatim translation can be perfectly accurate and utterly wrong. Take a phrase as ordinary as 'ni pukha ni pera', the traditional wish of good luck before an exam. Rendered word for word, it becomes 'neither down nor feather', which tells an English reader nothing at all. Rendered as 'good luck', it loses the superstition, the hunting origin and the ritual reply. Somewhere between the calque and the paraphrase lies the version I am looking for, and I do not always find it.\n\nThen there are false friends, those treacherous cognates that look familiar and mean something quite different. A Russian 'magazin' is a shop, not a magazine; a 'simpatichny' man is attractive rather than sympathetic. Every translator has a private collection of embarrassing mistranslations, usually discovered by an alert reader the week after publication.\n\nTheorists describe the central choice as one between domestication and foreignisation. Do I make the text feel as though it had been written in English, smoothing away anything strange? Or do I preserve its foreignness, asking the reader to meet the original halfway? Domesticate too much and you produce a novel in which Muscovites drink tea like vicars in an English village. Foreignise too much and you produce a clunky, footnote-heavy text that nobody finishes. Most of the time I try to strike a balance, and most of the time I err on the side of the reader.\n\nWordplay is where the job becomes almost impossible. A pun is untranslatable by definition, because it depends on an accident of one particular language. What I can do is find a different accident in English, one that produces a similar effect at a slightly different moment. Purists call this cheating. I call it the only honest option: if the author made the reader smile on page forty, my job is to make my reader smile somewhere near page forty, not to explain in a footnote why a Russian reader would have smiled.\n\nIs translation, then, a lesser art? I do not think so. To translate is to read more closely than any critic, to write more carefully than most novelists, and to accept, with good grace, that the best work you do will go unnoticed. When a reviewer praises the 'elegant prose' of a novel I have translated without mentioning my name, I am not unhappy. It means the illusion has worked. It means that, for a few hundred pages, nothing was lost in translation, or at least that nobody could see what was.",
