      minWords: 230, use: ["untranslatable", "strike a balance", "calque", "lost in translation"],
      model: "Is anything truly untranslatable? Ask a professional translator and you will probably hear a sigh, a laugh and a story. The sigh is for the words that have no equivalent; the laugh is for the mistakes we have all made; and the story is usually about a pun that refused to cross the border.\n\nTake the Russian word 'toska'. Nabokov famously argued that no single English word could render all its shades, from a dull restlessness to an agonising spiritual anguish. A translator can choose 'longing', 'anguish' or 'melancholy', but each choice illuminates one facet and hides the others. In that sense, the word is untranslatable. In another sense, it is perfectly translatable: a skilful writer can convey the feeling through context, rhythm and imagery, even if no single word does the job.\n\nThere are, broadly speaking, two temptations. The first is the calque, the word-for-word copy that preserves the shape of the original and kills its life. The second is free adaptation, which reads beautifully but quietly replaces the author's voice with the translator's. Neither is acceptable on its own. The art lies in knowing when to stay close and when to let go.\n\nSo what matters more, accuracy or vitality? I do believe the question is slightly misleading. A translation that is accurate but lifeless betrays the author just as surely as one that is lively but careless. The translator's task is to strike a balance, sentence by sentence, between the letter and the spirit.\n\nSomething will always be lost in translation. But something, if we are lucky, will also be found: a new rhythm, a new reader, a new life for a book that would otherwise have stayed silent."
