    text: "At the end of Wellington Street, wedged between a laundrette and a shop that sold phone cases, stood Mr Abel's bookshop. It had stood there for forty-one years, and for at least thirty of them, people had been predicting its closure.\n\nRarely did anyone buy anything. Students came in to shelter from the rain; tourists photographed the window display, a precarious tower of Penguin classics that Mr Abel rearranged every Monday; and a handful of regulars came to argue with him about Dostoevsky. He did not mind. Profitable the shop had never been, but it was, he liked to say, the only place in town where a person could still get lost on purpose.\n\nOn the morning the letter arrived, he had been reading Chekhov at the counter. The landlord, who had inherited the building from his father the previous winter, regretted to inform him that the rent would be tripled from the first of June. Mr Abel read the letter twice, folded it into the Chekhov as a bookmark and went on reading. Only later, when he reached the end of the story and found the letter waiting for him, did he allow himself to understand what it meant.\n\nHe told no one. Not his regulars, not his daughter in Leeds, not the girl from the café across the road who brought him a flat white every afternoon and refused to let him pay. Instead, he began, very quietly, to give the books away. A boy who lingered too long by the poetry shelf found a battered Keats slipped into his bag. A woman who had been coming in every Saturday for twenty years without ever buying anything was presented, without explanation, with a first edition of Middlemarch. 'It's been waiting for you,' he told her, which was, in its way, true.\n\nSo unusual was this behaviour that people began to talk. The regulars suspected illness; the girl from the café suspected romance. It was the boy with the Keats who finally asked him outright. Mr Abel considered lying, then decided that, at seventy-three, he had earned the right to be honest. He showed him the letter.\n\nWhat happened next he would describe, for the rest of his life, as the only plot twist he had ever believed in. Within a week, the boy had told his sister, who had told her friends at the university, whose students' union organised a reading marathon outside the shop. Within a month, a crowdfunding page had raised enough to buy the lease outright. Journalists came. A minor celebrity read Wordsworth through a megaphone. Mr Abel found the whole thing faintly mortifying and enormously moving, in roughly equal measure.\n\nThe shop is still there. The tower of Penguins still wobbles in the window. And on the wall behind the counter, framed, hangs the landlord's letter, with a single line added at the bottom in Mr Abel's careful hand: 'Not all endings are written by the author.'",
