    model: "Few novels are as difficult to translate as Mikhail Bulgakov's 'The Master and Margarita', and few reward the effort so richly. I recently reread it in English, partly out of curiosity and partly to test a suspicion: that much of its magic would not survive the journey.\n\nThe first thing that strikes the reader is the narrator. He is not a neutral voice but a mischievous, gossiping presence who addresses us directly, promises revelations and occasionally pretends to know less than he does. Not only does this voice hold together a plot that moves from Soviet Moscow to Pontius Pilate's Jerusalem, but it also gives the book its peculiar mixture of comedy and dread. At times the narrator sounds like a satirical columnist; at others, like a man speaking in a whisper at a funeral.\n\nBulgakov's great structural device is the juxtaposition of these two worlds. The Moscow chapters are noisy, absurd and crowded with petty bureaucrats; the Jerusalem chapters are written in sober, almost biblical prose. Seldom has a novel made such abrupt shifts of tone feel so inevitable. By the final chapters, the two strands have merged, and the reader realises that the novel has been asking the same question in both: what does it cost to be a coward?\n\nAs for the translation, it is careful and largely successful. The satirical passages are rendered with real wit, and many of the jokes land. Inevitably, some things are lost. The bureaucratic jargon of the 1930s, which a Russian reader recognises instantly, becomes simply odd in English, and certain puns have been quietly flattened. Strange though it may seem, however, these losses matter less than I expected. The narrator's voice survives, and with it the novel's soul.\n\nWhether you read it in Russian or in English, this is a book that repays rereading. Rarely does a novel make you laugh and think about cowardice on the same page."
